From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: launch programs without leading their session

A launched program was its terminal session's leader. When a session
leader exits, the kernel hangs up the foreground process group, so a
child forked just before the program exited died of SIGHUP before it
could call setsid(): daemonizing programs (Mirai and most Linux malware)
never got past their first fork. The program keeps the terminal as its
controlling terminal (Ctrl+C, /dev/tty and job control are unchanged)
but no longer leads the session, as when run from a terminal's shell.

---
 kernel/antinomie/control.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/antinomie/control.c b/kernel/antinomie/control.c
--- a/kernel/antinomie/control.c
+++ b/kernel/antinomie/control.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 #include <linux/pid.h>
 #include <linux/reboot.h>
 #include <linux/sched/signal.h>
+#include <linux/sched/task.h>
 #include <linux/sched/user.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/syscalls.h>
@@ -223,6 +224,14 @@
 		close_fd(fd);
 	if (error)
 		return error;
+	/* The terminal is now the session's controlling terminal. The program
+	 * keeps it, but does not lead the session, as under a terminal's shell:
+	 * a session leader's exit hangs up the foreground process group, which
+	 * would kill a child the program forked before that child could call
+	 * setsid() (a daemonizing program, most malware). */
+	write_lock_irq(&tasklist_lock);
+	current->signal->leader = 0;
+	write_unlock_irq(&tasklist_lock);
 	error = ant_monitor_launch(launch->path, launch->argv, launch->envp);
 	if (error)
 		return error;
-- 
